```liquid
{% comment %} Get cart size - assign first since filters aren't allowed in if {% endcomment %}
{% assign cart = {{custom_attribute.${cart_items}}} %}
{% assign cart_size = cart | size %}

{% if cart_size > 3 %}
  <h2>Your cart is filling up!</h2>

  {% for item in cart limit: 3 %}
    <p>{{item.name}} — ${{item.price}}</p>
  {% endfor %}

  {% comment %} Can't reference two custom attributes in one expression - assign one first {% endcomment %}
  {% assign rewards = {{custom_attribute.${rewards_balance}}} | default: 0 %}
  {% assign total_balance = {{custom_attribute.${giftcard_balance}}} | plus: rewards | default: 0 %}

  <p>Your total balance: ${{total_balance}}</p>
{% endif %}
```

**Key points:**

- **Assign before comparing** — Braze doesn't allow `| size` inside `{% if %}`, so we assign it first
- **Two custom attributes** — can't add them in one expression, so `rewards` is assigned first, then added to `giftcard_balance`
- **`limit: 3`** on the loop shows only the first 3 items
- **`default: 0`** ensures missing balances don't render blank

Test with **Preview as Custom User**, entering mock values for `cart_items` as a string array and numeric balances.

Here's how to set up Connected Content with abort guards for your product recommendation row:

```liquid
{% comment %} Fetch recommendations with retry on failure {% endcomment %}
{% connected_content https://api.example.com/recommendations?user={{${user_id}}}
   :retry
   :save recs %}

{% comment %} Abort if the API didn't return 200 {% endcomment %}
{% if recs.__http_status_code__ != 200 %}
  {% abort_message('recommendation API returned non-200') %}
{% endif %}

{% comment %} Abort if we got fewer than 3 products {% endcomment %}
{% assign product_count = recs.products | size %}
{% if product_count < 3 %}
  {% abort_message('fewer than 3 recommendations available') %}
{% endif %}

{% comment %} Render the 3-across row {% endcomment %}
<table role="presentation" style="width:100%">
  <tr>
    {% for item in recs.products limit: 3 %}
    <td style="width:33.33%; padding:10px; vertical-align:top">
      <img src="{{item.image_url}}" alt="{{item.name}}" style="max-width:100%; height:auto">
      <h3>{{item.name}}</h3>
      <p>${{item.price | divided_by: 100.0 | money}}</p>
      <a href="{{item.url}}">Shop now</a>
    </td>
    {% endfor %}
  </tr>
</table>
```

**Key pieces:**

- **`:retry`** — gives the endpoint 5 attempts with exponential backoff. If all fail, the message is aborted automatically.
- **`__http_status_code__`** — only available when the endpoint returns a JSON **object** (not an array). If your API returns an array at the top level, wrap it: `{"products": […]}`.
- **Static abort reasons** — the string must be in quotes with no Liquid inside it. Currents logs these as `liquid_abort_message` with your reason in `abort_log`.

**What happens when it aborts:**
- No send, no delivery record, no user profile entry
- Message Activity Log shows `{% abort_message('your reason') %} called`
- Currents logs `abort_type: "liquid_abort_message"`

**Testing:**
- Use **Preview & Test → Preview as Custom User** to bypass abort logic and see the rendered output
- On the **Test Send** tab, tick **"Override recipients' attributes with current preview user's attributes"** before sending a test
- To simulate the API being down, temporarily point the URL at a non-existent endpoint

One note: if you're passing a user identifier in the query string, consider moving it to a POST body where your endpoint supports it — query parameters land in access logs and Braze retries put them there repeatedly.
